# ===========================================================================
# apply-rollout settings -- template
#
# Author: Haris Ahmad -- Smart IS
#
# Copy this file to apply-rollout.conf beside the script and edit the values:
#
#     cp apply-rollout.conf.example apply-rollout.conf
#
# The file is READ, never sourced. No value can execute code or overwrite the
# tool's internal state. Unknown keys are ignored, so a settings file carrying
# keys for the other TYPE, or keys from an older version, does not break a run.
#
# An absent or empty file is fine. The tool then behaves as if no settings were
# given, which is deliberately the SAFE default: TYPE falls back to rollout,
# and rollout IS the no-conventions behaviour -- the manifest is taken
# literally and nothing is written that the package did not name.
#
# Format:   KEY=value
#   - comments (#) and blank lines are ignored
#   - surrounding quotes are stripped
#   - trailing slashes are normalised away
#   - Windows line endings are tolerated
# ===========================================================================


# ===========================================================================
# The two types, and why hotfix needs so few keys
#
# TYPE selects how a manifest path becomes a destination, and nothing else.
# Directive parsing, execution, collision detection, logging and exit status
# are identical under both.
#
#     rollout  the manifest is taken literally. Nothing is derived, so the
#              three REFSDIR_* keys are the only thing that can place the REFS
#              variable. This is the tool's ORIGINAL behaviour, and the
#              DEFAULT when TYPE is blank or absent.
#
#     hotfix   paths are corrected against conventions the target declares.
#              The REFSDIR_* keys are inert here; the list below is derived
#              instead.
#
# Under hotfix, everything the tool needs is DERIVED from the target repository
# at run time, by reading what the repository itself declares:
#
#     the refs root ............ the directory holding the shared patch config
#                                and build file
#     the patch source tree .... the source-dir the patch config declares
#     valid deploy kinds ....... the kinds the patch config's load target lists
#     the deployed-shape wrapper whatever sits between the deploy directory and
#                                the first segment naming a valid kind
#     asset kinds .............. the directories the build file copies out of
#     source kinds ............. the directories the config's includes read from
#     bundle target names ...... declared in the patch config
#     the copy destination ..... the expression the build file's own copy
#                                entries already write to
#     the deployed web tree .... named by the build file's copy destination
#     the upgrade version ...... the highest version directory present
#
# These are not settings because the target already declares them, and a stale
# setting would be worse than no setting. A platform rename is followed without
# touching this file.
#
# A derivation that finds nothing simply disables the convention it supports;
# resolution then falls back to the manifest. An unfamiliar target degrades to
# manifest-only placement rather than failing or guessing.
#
# Under rollout none of the above is read at all. A rollout run has no use for
# any of it, and deriving facts it would then ignore would invite them being
# consulted by accident.
# ===========================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYPE -- which resolution this run uses.
#
#   rollout        the manifest decides every destination (see above)
#   hotfix         paths are corrected against declared conventions
#   blank / absent rollout
#
# Matched without regard to case. An UNRECOGNISED VALUE IS A SETUP ERROR, exit
# 2, refused before the run creates so much as a log file -- never a silent
# fallback to the default. A misspelled type that quietly selected the other
# resolution would write the wrong tree while reporting success, which is the
# one failure this tool exists to remove.
#
# Set this EXPLICITLY on a destination that needs hotfix. Leaving it blank
# there does not preserve hotfix behaviour -- blank means rollout.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TYPE=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOG_DIR -- where run logs are written.
#
# This is a property of the MACHINE, not of the destination, which is why it is
# a setting at all.
#
#   absolute path  -> used as-is
#   relative path  -> placed beside the script
#   blank / absent -> the default log/ directory beside the script
#
# One file per run, timestamped, tagged with the active modes. A run never
# overwrites another run's log: concurrent runs starting in the same second
# each get their own numbered file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOG_DIR=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JAR_DIR -- destination for jars the manifest names in the GENERIC library
# directory.
#
# Two library locations are both legitimate, and a target may standardise on
# one. This key exists only for that choice.
#
#   blank / absent -> the manifest decides; jars land exactly where it says
#   set            -> a jar the manifest names as  lib/<name>.jar  goes here
#                     instead, keeping its own filename
#
# Never affects:
#   - paths the manifest already names in the java library directory: those
#     always land there, un-redirected
#   - anything in the generic library directory that is not a .jar
#
# Relative values are taken from the target directory; absolute values are used
# as-is.
#
# Note: if you point this at a directory the repository ignores, the jars are
# delivered but untracked. The run reports any such destination -- everything
# delivered must be trackable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JAR_DIR=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REFSDIR_ROOT / REFSDIR_WEB / REFSDIR_DEPLOY -- ROLLOUT ONLY.
#
# Where the REFS variable is placed. Under hotfix these are INERT: the refs
# root and the deployed-shape wrapper are derived from the target instead, and
# nothing is written to the deployed web tree at all. A run reports them as
# inert rather than failing, so one settings file can serve both types.
#
# The two subpaths are relative to the root, and each REPLACES the segment that
# selected it rather than nesting beneath it:
#
#     $REFSDIR/web/<rest>     ->  <target>/<ROOT>/<WEB>/<rest>
#     $REFSDIR/deploy/<rest>  ->  <target>/<ROOT>/<DEPLOY>/<rest>
#     $REFSDIR/<rest>         ->  <target>/<ROOT>/<rest>
#
# This is the original meaning of these three keys, unchanged, so a settings
# file written for the original tool still resolves exactly as it always did.
#
# The REFS variable is the ONLY one placed by settings. Every other directory
# variable maps to the target directory, so LES-side content is never moved.
#
#   REFSDIR_ROOT    blank -> webclient
#   REFSDIR_WEB     blank -> web
#   REFSDIR_DEPLOY  blank -> deploy
#
# With all three blank, $REFSDIR/web/a/b.js lands at
# <target>/webclient/web/a/b.js -- the plainest possible reading.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
REFSDIR_ROOT=
REFSDIR_WEB=
REFSDIR_DEPLOY=
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMBINE_PATHS -- base path(s) holding per-table load directories, used by
# MANUAL consolidation (--combine). Several paths may be given, space separated.
#
#   blank / absent -> --combine must be given paths on the command line,
#                     otherwise it stops with a setup error
#   set            -> --combine uses these when no paths are passed
#
# --combine ALSO requires --folders naming the table folders to rebuild. There is
# no "every folder under the base path" default, and manual consolidation never
# deletes a combined artifact.
#
# A convenience only, to spare repeating the same paths on every manual run.
# There is deliberately no default: guessing where load data lives is exactly
# the kind of inference this tool exists to remove.
#
# Automatic consolidation during apply and undo does NOT use this key -- it
# works from the directories the run actually touched.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMBINE_PATHS=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMBINE_EXCLUDE -- data trees that are delivered but never consolidated.
#
# A destination may keep more than one per-table data tree: records to LOAD and
# records to DELETE. They look identical -- a control file beside a directory of
# the same name -- and their table names collide, but their control files are
# opposite operations:
#
#     load tree    <table>.ctl : publish data where table = ...
#     delete tree  <table>.ctl : [delete from <table> where ...]
#
# Only one of them can own a given combined file in the version directory, and
# nothing in the destination says which. So the delete tree is named here.
#
#   blank / absent -> nothing is excluded
#   set            -> these space-separated path segments are excluded
#
# There is no built-in default. A tree name compiled into the tool would be
# wrong for every other destination and invisible to whoever runs against one,
# so an exclusion is stated here or it does not exist.
#
# The excluded tree's files are still delivered normally -- only its combined
# artifact is not built, and the run log says so every time. If you later give
# the delete tree a real destination of its own, remove it from this list.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMBINE_EXCLUDE=


# ===========================================================================
# Worked examples
#
# None of the values below are real -- copy the shape, not the names. Both
# assume the target's own root is /opt/app/LES, with db/, src/ and the rest
# sitting directly under it.
#
# A file this destination doesn't set at all keeps its safe default: an
# absent or partial settings file is a fully supported, ordinary way to run
# this tool, not a shortcut. Neither example below lists a key it has no use
# for.
#
# Rollout, with the webclient references kept under refs/ instead of the
# tool's default webclient/:
#
#     TYPE=rollout
#     REFSDIR_ROOT=refs
#     REFSDIR_WEB=web
#     REFSDIR_DEPLOY=deploy
#
# Reading it: rollout resolution, log files land in the default log/ next to
# the script, jars land wherever the manifest already says (JAR_DIR is not
# set), and $REFSDIR/web/a/b.js in a manifest now lands at
# /opt/app/LES/refs/web/a/b.js instead of the default
# /opt/app/LES/webclient/web/a/b.js.
#
# Hotfix, with one delete tree excluded from consolidation:
#
#     TYPE=hotfix
#     LOG_DIR=/var/log/rollout
# 
#
# Reading it: hotfix resolution, logs collected in one place across every
# destination on this machine, jars land wherever the manifest already says,
# and the widget_retired_queue tree is a delete tree at this destination --
# its files still get delivered, its combined artifact does not get built.
# REFSDIR_ROOT/WEB/DEPLOY are not set because they are inert under hotfix
# anyway. COMBINE_PATHS is not set either: it only saves typing a base path
# that --combine would otherwise take on the command line, so a destination
# that always passes it explicitly has no use for it.
# ===========================================================================
